WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.450
Hi, my name is Mateo Robles.

2
00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:03.990
I'm a computer science graduate

3
00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:05.720
and the founder of SyberLabs,

4
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:07.410
a small research studio.

5
00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:09.790
I build AI tools that people can trust,

6
00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:11.860
because every step leaves evidence someone

7
00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:12.900
can check.

8
00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.770
In the GitHits design partnership,

9
00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:17.170
I connected an AI coding tool to a service

10
00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:19.310
that finds relevant code examples.

11
00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:20.590
I built the connection,

12
00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:22.650
added safeguards so it could only make allowed

13
00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:25.550
requests, and kept a record of every call.

14
00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:28.250
Then I tested it: successful searches,

15
00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:29.800
requests that should be blocked,

16
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:31.550
and whether the records held up.

17
00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:33.390
The demo uses controlled test data,

18
00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:35.360
and public review feedback made the work

19
00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:36.360
stronger.

20
00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.170
GitHits fits into SyberWork,

21
00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:40.150
where contracts govern work.

22
00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:42.230
Every task keeps a case history of what

23
00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:43.950
was proposed, admitted,

24
00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:45.340
observed, approved,

25
00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:47.820
and executed, so people stay in charge

26
00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.000
of what ships.

27
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.400
Attest, which I built at the Kane CLI Hack

28
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:54.210
Day, makes documentation that can't lie.

29
00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.720
An agent runs every step a README claims,

30
00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:58.400
badges each one with evidence,

31
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.780
and patches the doc when a step fails.

32
00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:02.900
FLYSPACE is our workspace for people

33
00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:03.950
and agents.

34
00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:05.050
I built its compiler,

35
00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:07.860
which turns OpenAPI and MCP schemas into

36
00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.420
typed blocks on a canvas,

37
00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:12.210
and made agent writes wait for human approval.

38
00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:13.990
I work from first principles:

39
00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:15.430
start with the real goal,

40
00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:16.740
question assumptions,

41
00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:18.720
build the smallest useful version,

42
00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:19.600
check the evidence,

43
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:20.760
and improve it.

44
00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:23.110
My path from sociology to computer science

45
00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:25.030
taught me that coordinating AI agents

46
00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:27.030
is a lot like coordinating people.

47
00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:28.810
Clear roles, shared goals,

48
00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:31.420
context, and good handoffs matter.

49
00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:33.720
I also make Gyre, which turns mathematics

50
00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:35.210
into live browser art,

51
00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:36.530
and I help build RISE,

52
00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:38.420
which makes reading interactive.

53
00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:40.690
I'm looking for new grad software engineering,

54
00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:42.870
AI engineering, and creative technologist

55
00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:43.570
roles.

56
00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:45.280
If you want someone curious who follows

57
00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.180
through, invite me to interview.

58
00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.280
Let's build something useful together.
